{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nExtract bridge orchestration into a runner-neutral packaged Python core and keep provider workflows thin. The capability must work through all three public interfaces: Python library, CLI, and MCP. It must ship from all supported install paths: pip wheel, Homebrew's wheel-backed environment, and MCP Registry/uvx.\n\nThis is an additive migration. Existing checked-out workflows and environments that invoke `rebar reconcile --mode ...` keep their defaults, messages, mutation boundaries, and legacy benign results 3/4/75. At the provider boundary, the existing `MODE` environment variable remains supported; the new public operation uses the canonical noun `profile`.\n\nThe packaged runner accepts five profiles and preserves these routes:\n\n- `reconcile-check` invokes `rebar reconcile --mode reconcile-check`.\n- `dry-run` invokes `rebar bridge preview`.\n- `bootstrap-strict` invokes `rebar bridge sync --max-changes 10`.\n- `bootstrap-throttle` invokes `rebar bridge sync --max-changes 100`.\n- `live` invokes uncapped `rebar bridge sync`.\n\nThe Python core owns environment validation, the 3600-second timeout, pause-marker validation, provider-result translation, and strict ticket-store delivery. Direct legacy callers continue to observe 3/4/75; only the new provider runner maps benign legacy results to canonical success. GitHub Actions, Jenkins, and GitLab retain only provider-native checkout/bootstrap, credentials, signing-key materialization, artifacts/notifications, scheduling, and invocation of exactly `rebar bridge run`.\n\nGitLab portability is validated offline against a vendored Draft-07 schema with immutable upstream revision, retrieval date, dialect, and SHA-256 provenance. Shell embedded in provider configuration is linted with the exact pinned ShellCheck development dependency. No repository-local shell or Python runner script is shipped: behavior is in the wheel.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Inventory the existing workflow, compatibility contracts, and all parser/lint/policy consumers.\n2. Add RED-first happy, edge, end-to-end, surface-parity, and install-path tests; physically hold edge and end-to-end tests away from the implementation worker.\n3. Implement a packaged Python runner and expose it through `rebar.bridge_run`, `rebar bridge run --profile`, and a write-gated MCP `bridge_run` tool.\n4. Replace inline GitHub orchestration with the installed CLI and add native GitLab and Jenkins adapters that invoke the same CLI.\n5. Preserve provider `MODE` compatibility and direct legacy reconcile result behavior.\n6. Validate wheel contents and simulate pip, Homebrew wheel-backed, and MCP/uvx installations outside the repository.\n7. Validate provider schemas and embedded shell, then update generated references and user/release documentation.\n8. Run focused held-out suites, mutation checks, lint, typecheck, configuration checks, lock validation, and CI.\n\nNo contention harness is required: the runner invokes existing locked reconciler and store seams and adds no retry, cache, snapshot, or composed check-then-act store mutation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Packaged Python is the sole runner-neutral orchestration implementation; no repository-local runner script is required.\n- [ ] `rebar.bridge_run(profile=..., repo_root=...)` exposes the operation through the public Python library and returns captured structured output without writing streams.\n- [ ] `rebar bridge run --profile ...` exposes the same operation through the installed CLI and renders captured streams once.\n- [ ] MCP exposes the same operation as a typed write-gated tool, blocked by read-only mode and requiring Jira-sync authorization.\n- [ ] The wheel contains the runner core and proves availability from pip, Homebrew wheel-backed, and MCP Registry/uvx install shapes.\n- [ ] Explicit `repo_root` overrides inherited `REBAR_ROOT`; installed CLI use outside a repository honors configured `REBAR_ROOT`.\n- [ ] All five existing provider `MODE` values remain accepted and map to the specified legacy or canonical routes.\n- [ ] Public help, library, MCP, schemas, and docs use `profile`; provider configuration retains `MODE` compatibility.\n- [ ] Direct legacy reconcile callers retain published benign results 3/4/75.\n- [ ] The runner translates legacy benign results to canonical 0 while preserving operational 1 and invalid-invocation 2.\n- [ ] Exact valid pause markers skip delivery and return success; malformed markers do not silently pause.\n- [ ] Every successful non-paused pass invokes strict delivery with run id, bot identity, and `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always`; delivery failure returns 1 with diagnostics.\n- [ ] A shallow repository returns invalid invocation before reconciliation.\n- [ ] GitHub Actions, Jenkins, and GitLab invoke exactly `rebar bridge run`; GitHub keeps the 20-minute schedule and optional continuous redispatch.\n- [ ] Provider wrappers are schema-validated and their embedded shell passes the pinned ShellCheck; missing or mismatched tooling fails validation.\n- [ ] GitLab schema provenance records and verifies an immutable upstream revision and checksum.\n- [ ] Copied-workflow migration documentation requires atomic workflow and package-pin refresh, while old workflows on old pins remain supported.\n- [ ] Existing workflow, policy, partial-clone, identity, legacy compatibility, library, CLI, and MCP tests remain green.\n- [ ] No new ADR is added; therefore no ADR number or index entry is required."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786422741568422001,"uuid":"36eb56d1-3f16-44b7-8232-4190898d87b7"}